<h2>DORB export is free again — here's what changed</h2>
<p>India prohibited the export of de-oiled rice bran (DORB) from July 2023, as part of a broader set of measures to manage domestic feed and edible-oil-industry supply during a period of food-price sensitivity. That ban stayed in place for a little over two years. <strong>DGFT Notification No. 37/2025-26, dated 3 October 2025, lifted the ban</strong> and returned DORB to Free export status.</p>
<p>If you were sourcing DORB from India before 2023, or have been watching for the ban to lift, this is the update: Green Plus EXIM can now quote and supply DORB for export.</p>

<h2>DORB vs raw rice bran: don't conflate the two</h2>
<p>This distinction matters because the two products carry different export terms. <strong>De-oiled rice bran (DORB)</strong> — the defatted residue after oil is solvent-extracted from raw bran, classified under HS 2306.90 (and 2302.40 in some reporting) — is the product the October 2025 notification freed. <strong>Raw or boiled rice bran</strong> — a separate product under HS 2302.20, typically used as an input for oil extraction rather than as a finished feed ingredient — remains export-Restricted, permitted only under licence. If a supplier offers you "rice bran" without specifying which, ask — the two are not interchangeable commercially or regulatorily.</p>

<h2>Reading the trade data below — a caution</h2>
<p>The export figures in the table below cover calendar-2024, which falls entirely inside the export-ban window. The roughly USD 0.42 million total is a measure of the ban's effectiveness, not of real demand — genuine post-ban trade volumes aren't visible in Comtrade data yet as of this page's last update. Nepal, Bangladesh and other feed-import-dependent regional markets are the natural destinations once normal trade resumes, based on pre-ban trade patterns and regional feed demand, but treat any current DORB market-sizing claim (from us or anyone else) with appropriate skepticism until fresh post-ban data is available.</p>

<h2>How should I specify DORB?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Protein and fibre.</strong> 14–16% crude protein and up to 12–14% crude fibre are typical. Confirm your feed formulation's tolerance.</li>
  <li><strong>Sand/silica content.</strong> This varies meaningfully by processing quality — specify your maximum tolerance explicitly rather than accepting a generic "export quality" claim.</li>
  <li><strong>Freshness/turnover.</strong> DORB is more prone to rancidity than whole grains due to residual oil content — plan your shipment and storage timeline accordingly.</li>
</ul>

